          "**Saphenofemoral junction (SFJ)**: the GSV pierces the **cribriform fascia at the saphenous opening 2.5-4 cm below and lateral to the pubic tubercle** to join the femoral vein; its tributaries - **superficial epigastric, superficial circumflex iliac, superficial and deep external pudendal** - must all be ligated flush at surgery or recurrence follows. [Sabiston 22e]",
          "**Small saphenous vein (SSV)**: begins at the lateral end of the arch, passes **behind the lateral malleolus with the sural nerve**, ascends the midline of the calf and pierces the deep fascia to join the popliteal vein at a **variable saphenopopliteal junction (SPJ)** - hence duplex marking before surgery and the risk of sural and common peroneal nerve injury. [Gray's Anatomy 42e]",
          "**Perforating veins** carry blood **inward from superficial to deep** through one-way valves: **Dodd** (mid-thigh, Hunter canal), **Boyd** (below the knee), and the **medial calf (Cockett) or posterior tibial perforators** at about 7, 12 and 18 cm above the medial malleolus - these open directly onto the **gaiter area**, explaining where ulcers form. [Bailey and Love 28e]",
          "**Calf muscle pump (the peripheral heart)**: the soleal sinuses and deep veins in the tight fascial compartment are squeezed with each step, ejecting blood upward; valves stop backflow, so **ambulatory venous pressure falls from about 90 mmHg (standing still) to 20-30 mmHg on walking**. [Sabiston 22e]",
          "**Ambulatory venous hypertension** is the final common pathway: reflux through incompetent superficial, perforator or deep valves, deep venous obstruction or calf pump failure (immobility, ankle stiffness) keep venous pressure high during walking. [ESVS 2022]",
